WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.026
Hello!

00:00:02.026 --> 00:00:04.220
This is the "Mathematical Experiments" podcast.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.569
Today we will experiment with multiplication.

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:08.436
We'll draw these beautiful pictures.

00:00:08.436 --> 00:00:12.398
This picture is the multiplication table of 141 x 141.

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:13.709
This is multiplication by 2.

00:00:13.709 --> 00:00:15.126
This is multiplication by 3.

00:00:15.126 --> 00:00:16.251
By 6.

00:00:16.251 --> 00:00:18.128
This is some other number.

00:00:18.128 --> 00:00:20.469
And this seems to be multiplication by -2.

00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:25.877
Let me explain how we got this picture.

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:28.388
Take n points on a circle.

00:00:28.388 --> 00:00:30.837
Here, I took 41 points.

00:00:30.837 --> 00:00:35.024
Let's number them 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and so on.

00:00:35.024 --> 00:00:37.876
Let's do a simple action:

00:00:37.876 --> 00:00:39.425
connect 1 with 3,

00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:41.636
2 with 6.

00:00:41.636 --> 00:00:45.545
And let's connect point "x" with point "3x".

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.545
3 connects with 9,

00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:49.636
4 with 12,

00:00:49.636 --> 00:00:52.034
5 with 15, and so on.

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:54.871
12 connects with the number 36,

00:00:54.871 --> 00:00:57.428
13 with the number 39,

00:00:57.428 --> 00:01:01.390
and we need to connect 14 with the number 42,

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.106
but there is no number 42 here.

00:01:04.106 --> 00:01:09.950
You probably guessed it. We need to take the number 1 because 42 locates in the number 1.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.138
We are kind of numbering further

00:01:11.138 --> 00:01:12.000
40,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.012
41, 42, 43, 44, 45, 46, and so on around the circle.

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:23.584
Think that all natural numbers locate on this circle.

00:01:23.584 --> 00:01:30.922
This picture is different from the one on the right because it has 141 instead of 41.

00:01:32.062 --> 00:01:34.666
Now things get interesting.

00:01:34.666 --> 00:01:38.443
If you've seen the pictures above somewhere before,

00:01:38.443 --> 00:01:41.557
you're likely seeing these pictures for the first time.

00:01:41.557 --> 00:01:46.260
Okay... Let me try to explain in detail how to get these pictures.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.611
Let's draw them and take a look.

00:01:50.611 --> 00:01:52.232
Here's a big mouse.

00:01:52.232 --> 00:01:54.000
Here's a weirdo.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.708
Here's a star.

00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:57.230
And here's an eared one.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.344
It's some kind of coat of arms pattern.

00:02:00.344 --> 00:02:01.923
They are obtained in a similar way,

00:02:01.923 --> 00:02:09.438
only we connect not "x" and "3x", but two points x and y such that their product is 1.

00:02:09.438 --> 00:02:15.597
Let's do it. 3 * 14 = 42, and 42 locates in unity.

00:02:15.597 --> 00:02:20.274
2 * 21 also gives 42 and so on.

00:02:20.274 --> 00:02:28.452
Here all the segments are connected with pairs of x and y such that their product is 1.

00:02:28.452 --> 00:02:31.721
And here the same thing is done for the number 70,

00:02:31.721 --> 00:02:39.686
when we put 70 points on a circle and connect pairs whose product locates in the point 1.

00:02:41.542 --> 00:02:45.103
Here we have some unusual "whiskers"... let me explain.

00:02:45.103 --> 00:02:48.705
Some pairs of x and y consist of the same number x, that is x, x.

00:02:48.705 --> 00:02:52.716
For example, 1.
1*1 equals 1.

00:02:52.716 --> 00:03:00.693
Here, 40 * 40 also equals 1, because it's like -1 * -1.

00:03:03.424 --> 00:03:04.559
Moving on.

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:08.817
So we got these segments that connect inverse elements.

00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:10.260
What do we do next?

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:18.252
Next, we simply move along the circle and take these segments as they appear.

00:03:18.252 --> 00:03:21.869
First, we take the segment that connects 2 and 21.

00:03:21.869 --> 00:03:23.710
This segment is here.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.958
Then we take the segment that connects 3 and 14.

00:03:26.958 --> 00:03:27.746
This segment is here.

00:03:27.746 --> 00:03:29.534
It's like very reduced here.

00:03:29.534 --> 00:03:33.072
Here it's big, and here it's small.

00:03:34.345 --> 00:03:37.856
Then we come to the number 4.

00:03:37.856 --> 00:03:40.636
From 4, the segment goes to 31.

00:03:40.636 --> 00:03:41.666
This segment here.

00:03:41.666 --> 00:03:42.744
And so on.

00:03:42.744 --> 00:03:44.867
So we move along the circle,

00:03:44.867 --> 00:03:47.727
encounter the end of the segment and draw it,

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:50.257
starting from the point where we are at the moment.

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:55.372
As a result, we get this closed curve.

00:03:55.372 --> 00:03:57.293
A closed broken line.

00:03:57.293 --> 00:03:59.552
Why is it closed?

00:03:59.552 --> 00:04:00.347
It's very simple here.

00:04:00.347 --> 00:04:04.495
We take each segment twice, first in one direction, then in the other.

00:04:04.495 --> 00:04:08.245
And that's why in the end we will end up in the same point where we started.

00:04:09.147 --> 00:04:11.796
Well, here... look.

00:04:11.796 --> 00:04:14.512
First comes this segment.

00:04:14.512 --> 00:04:15.982
Here it is.

00:04:15.982 --> 00:04:18.168
Then this segment.

00:04:18.168 --> 00:04:20.168
Here it is.

00:04:20.168 --> 00:04:25.581
The next segment from 11 to some number (not visible here).

00:04:25.581 --> 00:04:26.971
Here it is.

00:04:26.971 --> 00:04:28.766
And so on.

00:04:32.186 --> 00:04:35.274
And here is the image for the number 213.

00:04:36.547 --> 00:04:39.646
Next, I want to show the code that can generate all of this.

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:42.680
You can quickly look through it without stopping,

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:47.332
and then, if desired, go back and meditate on individual pieces of code.

00:04:48.419 --> 00:04:50.419
This is how you can draw a circle.

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:54.911
If desired, you can specify the center and radius of the circle.

00:04:54.911 --> 00:04:57.598
By default, it has the value [{0, 0}, 1].

00:04:58.553 --> 00:05:01.456
I draw the points using the ListPlot function,

00:05:01.456 --> 00:05:03.907
and the coordinate pairs using the Table function.

00:05:03.907 --> 00:05:07.321
And a pair of coordinates on the circle is the cosine and sine.

00:05:07.321 --> 00:05:10.090
In fact, this is the definition of the cosine and sine functions.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:13.409
These are the coordinates of a point on the circle,

00:05:13.409 --> 00:05:19.598
where φ is the length of the rope that we tied to the point on the far right.

00:05:19.598 --> 00:05:21.598
We stretched this rope along the circle.

00:05:21.598 --> 00:05:29.894
We stopped when the rope ran out, and looked at the coordinates of the points - these are the coordinates of cos(φ) and sin(φ).

00:05:29.894 --> 00:05:36.000
Here below is a visualization of this idea using ParametricPlot.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.182
The length of the rope is controlled by a slider here.

00:05:41.182 --> 00:05:47.026
For clarity, the radius is constantly slightly increasing.

00:05:47.026 --> 00:05:49.544
And we see individual coils.

00:05:49.544 --> 00:05:54.000
But in reality, imagine that the rope is infinitely thin and is wound directly on the circle.

00:05:55.352 --> 00:05:58.308
The next point...

00:05:58.308 --> 00:06:03.516
Well, this is also a visualization of how points gradually appear as we move the slider.

00:06:03.516 --> 00:06:08.256
If desired, you can put labels on each point.

00:06:08.256 --> 00:06:12.496
This is done using the Labeled function.

00:06:12.496 --> 00:06:17.307
So we're gradually moving forward...

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:22.495
Here's a trick, like this.

00:06:22.495 --> 00:06:27.226
If you remove it, you get the following picture...

00:06:28.499 --> 00:06:32.077
We'll keep moving, moving...

00:06:33.588 --> 00:06:38.796
And on the second loop, new labels won't appear, but old ones will disappear.

00:06:38.796 --> 00:06:44.959
The thing is, points are drawn on top of each other and it doesn't know which label to draw.

00:06:44.959 --> 00:06:51.590
To prevent this, we slightly change the coordinates of the points that belong to the second loop.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:55.075
And it turns out well.

00:06:58.045 --> 00:07:02.322
It somehow chooses the location of these labels in a less than ideal way.

00:07:02.322 --> 00:07:06.608
The Mod function is responsible for calculating the remainder of division in Mathematica.

00:07:06.608 --> 00:07:10.676
Mod[m, n] gives the remainder of dividing m by n.

00:07:10.676 --> 00:07:13.578
Let's take a look at these remainders.

00:07:18.817 --> 00:07:21.378
The remainder of dividing 17 by 5.

00:07:21.378 --> 00:07:28.655
It's like finding the nearest number that is divisible by 5 (which is 15) and seeing how much needs to be added to 15 to get 17.

00:07:28.655 --> 00:07:31.555
2. Answer: 2.

00:07:31.555 --> 00:07:36.000
When you want to see what the remainder of dividing the product of two numbers is,

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.814
you can do this trick,

00:07:37.814 --> 00:07:44.834
instead of the original (larger) numbers, take the remainders of division and multiply the remainders themselves.

00:07:44.834 --> 00:07:49.102
They are usually smaller and therefore require less computation.

00:07:49.102 --> 00:07:50.851
The same answer will be obtained.

00:07:50.851 --> 00:07:58.790
So instead of the actual numbers, we could take their remainders when divided by 5.

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:06.631
This is especially clear when you act in modular arithmetic modulo 1000 or 100.

00:08:06.631 --> 00:08:12.249
The thing is, the remainder of division by 100 is the last two digits of the decimal representation.

00:08:12.249 --> 00:08:15.014
1000 is the last three digits.

00:08:15.014 --> 00:08:20.706
And most people know that to find the last three digits of a product,

00:08:20.706 --> 00:08:25.554
you just need to take the last three digits of each number and multiply them.

00:08:25.554 --> 00:08:27.804
Here are two large numbers.

00:08:27.804 --> 00:08:31.008
We multiplied them and took the remainder of division by 1000.

00:08:31.008 --> 00:08:35.010
Then we multiplied the remainders of division by 1000.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:38.981
And took the remainder of division of the product of remainders.

00:08:38.981 --> 00:08:43.046
All four expressions end in 236.

00:08:43.046 --> 00:08:46.704
Let's write this out in words.

00:08:46.704 --> 00:08:51.498
Big numbers a and b, small numbers as, bs.

00:08:51.498 --> 00:08:56.221
The remainder of division of the product of big numbers is equal to the remainder of the product of small numbers.

00:08:56.221 --> 00:08:59.130
This is how you can write it.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:00.348
Well... clear.

00:09:00.348 --> 00:09:03.099
Here it took specific values of a, b, and p.

00:09:03.099 --> 00:09:07.070
So what it wrote as True is not that interesting.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.933
This is true for specific three numbers.

00:09:08.933 --> 00:09:10.933
But is it true in general?

00:09:10.933 --> 00:09:13.402
You can also ask such questions in Mathematica.

00:09:13.402 --> 00:09:16.810
To do this, you need to use the Reduce function.

00:09:17.874 --> 00:09:21.689
Reduce takes an expression as its first argument,

00:09:21.689 --> 00:09:23.001
then you need to specify the variables,

00:09:23.001 --> 00:09:28.068
for which you want to simplify this expression,

00:09:28.068 --> 00:09:32.509
and specify the range of numbers in which you act.

00:09:32.509 --> 00:09:36.315
I wrote Integers here, which means a and b are integers.

00:09:36.315 --> 00:09:39.222
And it gave me this answer:

00:09:39.222 --> 00:09:42.442
both a and b belong to integers.

00:09:42.442 --> 00:09:45.149
This letter "Z" means integers.

00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:47.525
This symbol is read as "belongs to".

00:09:47.525 --> 00:09:50.388
The vertical bar is an alternative.

00:09:50.388 --> 00:09:52.732
It can be read as:

00:09:52.732 --> 00:09:56.891
each of the numbers a and b belongs to the integers.

00:09:56.891 --> 00:10:01.081
This is all that is required for this identity to hold.

00:10:01.081 --> 00:10:04.616
That is, it is true for any numbers a and b.

00:10:04.616 --> 00:10:11.820
Here's an alternative way to show why it's enough to take remainders when you're computing a product's remainder.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.514
Let's say there are two numbers:

00:10:13.514 --> 00:10:20.287
one has a remainder k1, that is, this number is expressed as k1 plus something that is divisible by 5,

00:10:20.287 --> 00:10:26.701
and the other number has a remainder k2, and it is expressed as k2 plus something that is divisible by 5.

00:10:26.701 --> 00:10:36.337
Then, after we expand the brackets, it's evident that the result contains k1 * k2 plus something that is divisible by 5.

00:10:36.337 --> 00:10:39.951
And we're not interested in that. We're only interested in k1 * k2.

00:10:39.951 --> 00:10:45.610
When we want to calculate the remainder of dividing all of this expression by 5.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.563
Let's check if Reduce can help us here.

00:10:48.563 --> 00:10:53.718
Yes, Mathematica can also simplify such expressions.

00:10:53.718 --> 00:10:56.301
Finally, let's move on to the multiplication table.

00:10:56.301 --> 00:10:58.301
Let's start with the addition table.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:03.122
Here's the addition table modulo 5.

00:11:03.122 --> 00:11:07.492
You can put 19 here and the table will be bigger.

00:11:07.492 --> 00:11:10.412
The addition table looks very simple.

00:11:10.412 --> 00:11:14.560
The same numbers are on the diagonals.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.549
There are no numbers greater than 18 in this table.

00:11:18.549 --> 00:11:20.339
That's understandable.

00:11:20.339 --> 00:11:24.901
Now, the multiplication table.

00:11:29.488 --> 00:11:31.513
Multiplying by 0 gives 0.

00:11:31.513 --> 00:11:35.581
Multiplying by 1 leaves all numbers as they are.

00:11:35.581 --> 00:11:40.854
It can be seen that the table is symmetric with respect to the diagonal.

00:11:42.636 --> 00:11:47.078
Let me try to define a numerical field using these two tables.

00:11:47.078 --> 00:11:50.144
The addition table and the multiplication table.

00:11:50.144 --> 00:11:53.004
Let's say we have a finite set of elements.

00:11:53.004 --> 00:11:56.037
I will define a finite numerical field for them.

00:11:56.037 --> 00:12:00.000
Let's say we have some finite set of elements.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.169
And someone has provided addition and multiplication tables for them.

00:12:04.169 --> 00:12:05.825
By hand.

00:12:05.825 --> 00:12:13.927
And I say, if these tables are good enough, then this set of elements can be called a numerical field.

00:12:13.927 --> 00:12:19.064
What does that mean?
What does "good enough" mean?

00:12:19.064 --> 00:12:24.756
First of all, both tables must be symmetric with respect to the diagonal.

00:12:24.756 --> 00:12:30.000
If we reflect with respect to this diagonal, we get the same table.

00:12:31.379 --> 00:12:33.384
The second point is:

00:12:33.384 --> 00:12:36.511
two elements must be selected: 0 and 1

00:12:36.511 --> 00:12:38.229
We will call 1 "zero",

00:12:38.229 --> 00:12:40.292
the other element we will call "one".

00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:45.513
Zero is an element that, when added to x, gives x.

00:12:45.513 --> 00:12:47.205
Let it be the first element.

00:12:47.205 --> 00:12:52.740
This means that the first row in the addition table should repeat the elements themselves,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:58.151
which we implicitly arranged at the top and left.

00:12:58.151 --> 00:13:01.090
The second highlighted element is one.

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:03.497
Multiplying by one should repeat everything.

00:13:03.497 --> 00:13:08.908
Let it be the second element, which means that in the second row all the elements should be repeated,

00:13:08.908 --> 00:13:12.442
which are implicitly arranged up here.

00:13:12.442 --> 00:13:15.318
Since both tables are symmetric with respect to the diagonal,

00:13:15.318 --> 00:13:22.262
this means that the second column repeats all the elements that are here on the left.

00:13:22.262 --> 00:13:24.424
The next important property:

00:13:24.424 --> 00:13:26.424
is that multiplying by 0 gives 0.

00:13:26.424 --> 00:13:33.152
This means that the first row and column of the multiplication table are entirely zeros.

00:13:33.152 --> 00:13:36.577
Such an unusual definition for tables.

00:13:36.577 --> 00:13:39.772
Tables must be symmetric with respect to the diagonal.

00:13:39.772 --> 00:13:43.837
The multiplication table should consist of the first row of zeros,

00:13:43.837 --> 00:13:46.139
the second row repeating all elements.

00:13:46.139 --> 00:13:52.050
And the addition table should have a repetition of all elements in the first row.

00:13:53.552 --> 00:13:55.518
And that's not all.

00:13:55.518 --> 00:13:59.708
It is also necessary for each row to contain all the elements.

00:13:59.708 --> 00:14:08.874
In both the addition and multiplication tables, each row and column must contain all field's elements.

00:14:08.874 --> 00:14:13.674
There is one more important property that cannot be expressed/described using tables.

00:14:13.674 --> 00:14:15.677
This is the distributivity property.

00:14:15.677 --> 00:14:18.209
Here it is written at the bottom.
(here is a mistake, right will be: a(b + c) = ab + ac)

00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:24.882
If addition and multiplication are such that this identity holds,
(here is a mistake, right will be: a(b + c) = ab + ac)

00:14:24.882 --> 00:14:31.951
then, together with the previous properties, we obtain a numerical field.

00:14:31.951 --> 00:14:37.354
Let's write the addition table in color.

00:14:37.354 --> 00:14:41.513
To do this, we will use the ArrayPlot function.

00:14:41.513 --> 00:14:47.612
As we increase the value of P, we get this image.

00:14:50.021 --> 00:14:52.661
Let's recall what our addition table looked like.

00:14:52.661 --> 00:14:56.873
There was a gradual increase as we approached this diagonal,

00:14:56.873 --> 00:15:03.035
then a switch to zero and growth again.

00:15:03.035 --> 00:15:06.287
Here, our zero corresponds to white,

00:15:06.287 --> 00:15:11.385
and 18, if we live modulo 19, to black.

00:15:12.699 --> 00:15:17.756
Here are zeros, ones, twos, threes, and so on.

00:15:18.663 --> 00:15:21.462
We can do the following trick.

00:15:21.462 --> 00:15:24.448
These numbers are arranged on a circle.

00:15:25.105 --> 00:15:28.560
And the numbers 18 and 0 are very close.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.655
I would like their colors to be close as well.

00:15:31.655 --> 00:15:36.468
To do this, I use ColorFunction and define it as follows.

00:15:37.692 --> 00:15:40.510
Hold on a sec... Let me first show you the result.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:45.817
There. I made it so that 18 and 0 have similar colors - green.

00:15:46.599 --> 00:15:48.231
This was done as follows:

00:15:48.231 --> 00:15:51.639
I wrote that ColorFunction is RGBColor,

00:15:51.639 --> 00:15:57.550
which takes the amount of red, green, and blue components.

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:01.230
As a hash, a number from zero to one is substituted, it seems.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:05.264
Zero corresponds to the minimum value, and one to the maximum.

00:16:05.264 --> 00:16:09.611
So I made that the color red is Sin[Pi#]^2,

00:16:09.611 --> 00:16:13.739
and green is Cos[2Pi#]^2.

00:16:13.739 --> 00:16:17.358
I got such a color profile.

00:16:17.358 --> 00:16:24.333
Let's fix this function and use it to get an image of multiplication.

00:16:24.333 --> 00:16:27.114
A multiplication table in color.

00:16:27.114 --> 00:16:29.351
This number is 104...

00:16:29.351 --> 00:16:30.699
105...

00:16:30.699 --> 00:16:32.699
Let's move step by step.

00:16:35.046 --> 00:16:39.136
For even numbers, we have a green cross in the center.

00:16:42.265 --> 00:16:46.581
For odd numbers, it's purple.

00:16:46.581 --> 00:16:52.974
As the number P increases, the picture becomes thinner and thinner.

00:16:55.133 --> 00:16:56.561
Okay!

00:16:56.561 --> 00:17:03.246
In addition to the addition and multiplication tables, there is one more very interesting table - the power table.

00:17:03.246 --> 00:17:05.056
Let's draw it.

00:17:06.276 --> 00:17:07.558
Here it is.

00:17:08.497 --> 00:17:11.055
It is structured as follows:

00:17:11.055 --> 00:17:15.621
at the top are numbers that correspond to points on the circle.

00:17:15.621 --> 00:17:19.248
Here we placed 13 points on the circle.

00:17:19.248 --> 00:17:22.152
And then we do the following operation:

00:17:22.152 --> 00:17:25.873
for example, the column for two.

00:17:25.873 --> 00:17:30.000
We multiply each subsequent element by 2.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.317
2 * 2 = 4, 4 * 2 = 8, 8 * 2 = 16

00:17:34.317 --> 00:17:38.195
16 on the circle, where 13 points is 3.

00:17:38.195 --> 00:17:39.100
3.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:42.000
Then we multiply by 2 again - 6.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.470
And so on...

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:47.401
Here for three, it's like powers of three.

00:17:48.559 --> 00:17:50.915
Here are powers of four.

00:17:51.917 --> 00:17:53.637
What do we see?

00:17:54.263 --> 00:17:55.721
We see a pattern.

00:17:55.721 --> 00:18:01.820
In the last row, which is -1, there are all ones.

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.383
And, by the way, if we continue the powers,

00:18:04.383 --> 00:18:10.357
(This j is our variable. Let's say we write +5 lines.)

00:18:10.357 --> 00:18:18.188
then we will see that it continues repeating 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, etc... The first row repeats.

00:18:18.188 --> 00:18:22.435
That's why I stopped at P - 1.

00:18:22.435 --> 00:18:24.779
There's no point in drawing further.

00:18:24.779 --> 00:18:27.937
Let's draw a power table in color.

00:18:27.937 --> 00:18:30.782
Just add a little manipulation:

00:18:30.782 --> 00:18:34.879
We multiply I^j by a and add b.

00:18:34.879 --> 00:18:44.449
When a = 1 and b = 0, it's just i^j.

00:18:44.449 --> 00:18:46.324
Here's what we see.

00:18:47.857 --> 00:18:54.125
Modulo 67, we see a picture with some structure.

00:18:54.125 --> 00:18:58.487
Here, we can see a bright green solid stripe.

00:18:59.019 --> 00:19:09.525
If we start increasing P, we get all sorts of patterns.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:16.323
When P is prime, the pattern is not so regular.

00:19:16.323 --> 00:19:22.383
The only thing we see for prime P is a horizontal line somewhere in the middle.

00:19:22.383 --> 00:19:30.000
But if we change "a", then sometimes we can see some additional structure.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.531
Like here.

00:19:31.531 --> 00:19:37.223
Separating horizontal lines and two vertical ones.

00:19:37.223 --> 00:19:47.356
That's to say, the multiplication by "a" somehow changes the palette and allows us to see the structure.

00:19:47.356 --> 00:19:51.171
Changing "b" is a simpler thing.

00:19:51.171 --> 00:19:55.768
It just cycles through the palette.

00:19:56.977 --> 00:20:00.000
"a" acts more complexly.

00:20:03.692 --> 00:20:09.220
It is the "a" that allows us to see the hidden structure.

00:20:10.847 --> 00:20:15.182
Let's look at our table of powers again in numbers.

00:20:15.182 --> 00:20:19.041
What is that line in the middle for prime numbers?

00:20:20.762 --> 00:20:25.258
Here it is. Here we have the numbers 1 and 18.

00:20:26.854 --> 00:20:27.698
Alright.

00:20:27.698 --> 00:20:35.781
Let's define the function Mod2, which will return -1 instead of 18.

00:20:35.781 --> 00:20:43.288
Because 18 just corresponds to moving in another direction by one segment.

00:20:44.634 --> 00:20:47.169
Let's calculate the Mod2 remainders.

00:20:47.169 --> 00:20:55.170
These are again remainders of division by 19, only instead of large remainders we see remainders with a minus sign.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:59.079
Mod2 chooses the remainder with the minimum absolute value.

00:20:59.079 --> 00:21:01.235
With a plus or minus sign.

00:21:02.576 --> 00:21:06.782
Let's draw a table of powers using Mod2.

00:21:06.782 --> 00:21:09.470
And here we see ones and minus ones,

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:12.000
and here ones.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:15.473
Thus, we have discovered two facts:

00:21:15.473 --> 00:21:22.402
The P - 1 row in the table of powers consists of ones - this is called Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:21:22.402 --> 00:21:28.671
And the P - 1/2 row in the table of powers consists of ones and minus ones.

00:21:28.671 --> 00:21:33.112
We will come back to these statements and perhaps even prove them.

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:40.226
The use of the Mod2 function allows us to see a simple, obvious, and in principle, regularity.

00:21:40.226 --> 00:21:49.892
If we ignore the minus signs in this table, we will see that the lower part of the table repeats the upper part.

00:21:49.892 --> 00:21:53.670
And indeed. Here are 3, 9, 8, 5.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:58.486
Here are -3, -9, -8, -5.

00:21:58.486 --> 00:22:03.056
These are opposite to the numbers that give -1 in the middle.

00:22:03.056 --> 00:22:05.400
And those that in the middle give one away...

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:10.936
already, there was a repetition without discarding -1.

00:22:10.936 --> 00:22:19.758
The sequence 4, -3, 7, 9 is repeated by the sequence that follows the line P - 1/2.

00:22:19.758 --> 00:22:23.481
4, -3, 7, 9

00:22:23.481 --> 00:22:26.838
We will come back to the study of power tables later.

00:22:26.838 --> 00:22:32.374
But now let's move on to another visualization of multiplication.

00:22:32.374 --> 00:22:37.597
There is a video on YouTube: Times Tables, Mandelbrot and the Heart of Mathematics.

00:22:37.597 --> 00:22:40.497
I recommend everyone to watch it.

00:22:40.497 --> 00:22:44.343
That's where this visualization is actually shown.

00:22:44.343 --> 00:22:46.969
Let's repeat it.

00:22:46.969 --> 00:22:50.303
Let's start by visualizing the addition of three.

00:22:50.303 --> 00:22:53.201
Let's say we have a number n.

00:22:53.201 --> 00:22:56.015
It is represented by this point.

00:22:56.015 --> 00:22:59.437
Let m be equal to n + k.

00:22:59.437 --> 00:23:01.656
It is represented by this point.

00:23:01.656 --> 00:23:08.481
By the way, we didn't need to write Mod here because Cos and Sin themselves perform the mod operation.

00:23:08.481 --> 00:23:10.356
Implicitly.

00:23:10.356 --> 00:23:16.422
Next, we output the segment (p1, p2).
p1 is a point. p2 is a point.

00:23:16.422 --> 00:23:23.649
And we perform this operation (calculating the segment (p1, p2)) for the most different values of n.

00:23:23.649 --> 00:23:26.211
This is what we get.

00:23:26.211 --> 00:23:32.748
Here's the point n. We added 3 to it and got the point m.

00:23:33.655 --> 00:23:39.781
Let's repeat this operation, but manipulate the variable k.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.485
Increase k...

00:23:45.674 --> 00:23:47.395
we get these images.

00:23:48.146 --> 00:23:49.794
We can also increase p.

00:23:54.175 --> 00:23:56.315
And this is what we get.

00:23:58.474 --> 00:24:00.324
Moving on.

00:24:01.357 --> 00:24:03.205
Next up, multiplication.

00:24:04.551 --> 00:24:07.455
We replace addition with multiplication...

00:24:07.455 --> 00:24:08.860
And draw.

00:24:08.860 --> 00:24:12.832
Here's multiplication by 3 modulo 17.

00:24:15.523 --> 00:24:18.429
We put it inside the Manipulate function.

00:24:18.429 --> 00:24:25.664
Here we allow k to be a decimal and step by 0.1 instead of 1.

00:24:25.664 --> 00:24:28.040
Let's see what we get.

00:24:29.292 --> 00:24:31.023
We increase...

00:24:31.023 --> 00:24:33.399
Gradually increase k...

00:24:34.807 --> 00:24:37.267
We see the birth of a cardioid...

00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:39.719
We got a cardioid.

00:24:40.032 --> 00:24:41.719
Move on...

00:24:51.013 --> 00:24:55.196
We went through the nephroid...

00:24:57.167 --> 00:24:59.840
Here's the curve with three petals.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:12.000
We can run Animate and get an animation of this process.

00:25:14.065 --> 00:25:16.707
Although it happens too fast.

00:25:17.709 --> 00:25:20.793
You can play with these pictures yourself.

00:25:20.793 --> 00:25:23.200
Check out the links in the description.

00:25:28.144 --> 00:25:30.947
Let's choose a triplet.

00:25:32.418 --> 00:25:37.651
And gradually increase p, starting with the number 10.

00:25:52.076 --> 00:25:54.800
A nephroid gradually appears.

00:26:02.717 --> 00:26:05.459
And here's the same thing but with labels.

00:26:12.656 --> 00:26:17.569
There's a video on YouTube showing an animation of gradually increasing the number k.

00:26:17.569 --> 00:26:20.178
It's very beautiful! I recommend it to everyone.

00:26:20.178 --> 00:26:22.247
The link is in the description.

00:26:22.247 --> 00:26:23.844
That's all for today.

00:26:23.844 --> 00:26:27.252
In the next video, we'll learn how to draw these pictures.

00:26:27.252 --> 00:26:29.188
We'll also learn what the inverse element is

00:26:29.188 --> 00:26:34.493
and prove that if p is prime, then there is an inverse element for any number n.

00:26:34.493 --> 00:26:39.807
We'll also get to know the Legendre symbol and learn how to compute it.

00:26:39.807 --> 00:26:41.900
Goodbye!

